== 9. What the plan said versus what the files show (11-26-2026 plan, Gemini)
VERDICT  FSC 'R_v1 (343 items, 0-5 ordinal), ~5 domain experts/item': not supported. Old set: 75 artifacts over 25 tasks with 195 expert ratings (2 ratings x 30/3 ratings x 45); new set: 100 artifacts, 247 ratings; 345 ratings over five raters is cited from the sister repo; 343 is in none of those files
VERDICT  DICES '60-112 raters/item': not what the files show. DICES-350: 123 ratings per item (104 after the README filter); DICES-990: 69 to 76 (60 to 69 after the filter)
VERDICT  ChaosNLI '3-class (4.6k items)': the three-class set has 3113 items; 4645 counts the 1532 binary abductive-NLI items as well
VERDICT  POPQUORN 'politeness avg 6.7 and offensiveness avg 8.7 raters/item': supported: files give 6.74 and 8.69 (maximum 8 and 10 per item, so a k-sweep past 8 to 10 is not possible)
VERDICT  POPQUORN '$12/hr, ~13 min per 50 ratings = ~$0.05/rating': supported as arithmetic on two sentences of the paper; the 13 minutes is for offensiveness only
VERDICT  HelpSteer2 'filtered out high-disagreement items >2 points': imprecise: the 2-point figure is real, but in the card it is the helpfulness agreement rule that decides whether extra annotators (up to five) are collected; the released label is the mean of the three most-agreeing annotators; the card describes no item filter based on disagreement (a quality-assurance step keeps 20,324 samples and names no disagreement criterion); per-annotator lists are released in the disagreements split
VERDICT  Kohli 'already runs subsampling saturation curves on ChaosNLI and DICES-990': supported by the paper's text; its 'correlation' is entropy correlation, and it compares soft-label, smoothed and hard-label training, not Pearson-trained judges

